{"answers": [{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"Entity: PDB 1acc is the crystal structure of Bacillus anthracis protective antigen (PA83; Petosa et al., 1997). It is the cell-binding and translocation component of anthrax toxin. Structurally, chain A has four parts: domain 1, which contains the PA20 fragment and a Ca2+-binding region and is the source of the 'PA14' domain name; domain 2, the β-barrel heptamerization and pore-forming domain with the membrane-insertion loop; domain 3, a small domain; and domain 4, the C-terminal receptor-binding β-sandwich. The fold and family placements and identifiers below come from memory, not from the release files, so check them in the SCOPe parseable files for each release.

SCOP 1.75: chain A was treated as one whole-chain domain, d1acca_. It sat in class f (membrane and cell-surface proteins) in a dedicated anthrax protective antigen fold, superfamily and family (as I recall, f.11 / f.11.1 / f.11.1.1). Because that lineage contained only PA, the classification said nothing about how PA's parts relate to other proteins.

Newer SCOPe releases: curators re-examined chain A and split it into several domains (sids of the form d1acca1, d1acca2, … replacing d1acca_), so each structural module gets its own classification. The key result is that the N-terminal module is recognized as the PA14 domain, a widespread β-sandwich module. It also occurs in bacterial glycosidases (e.g., β-glucosidases), adhesins, yeast flocculins and adhesins (Flo/Epa-type), proteases and}
